### The Problem with Traditional Caching

Implementing a robust two-tier caching architecture in .NET traditionally meant hand-coding a complex dance between `IMemoryCache` and `IDistributedCache` (usually Redis). Engineers had to manually manage serialization overhead, implement double-checked locking to prevent cache stampedes, and write verbose invalidation logic across boundaries. This boilerplate frequently introduces race conditions under high concurrent load.

### Enter HybridCache in .NET 9

.NET 9 introduces `HybridCache`, a unified abstraction designed to replace both `IMemoryCache` and `IDistributedCache`. It transparently handles L1 in-process RAM cache alongside L2 distributed Redis caching out of the box, while baking in robust protection against cache stampedes natively.

### Before: Custom Two-Tier Boilerplate

```csharp
var cached = await _redis.GetAsync(key);
if (cached is null) {
  await _lock.WaitAsync();
  cached = await _db.FetchAsync();
  await _redis.SetAsync(key, cached);
}
```

### After: Clean HybridCache API

```csharp
var item = await _cache.GetOrCreateAsync(
  key,
  async cancel => await _db.FetchAsync(),
  tags: ["orders"],
  cancellationToken: token);
return item;
```

### Why It Matters

• **Cache Stampede Protection:** Built-in coordination locks guarantee that only one concurrent request hits your database when a key expires, saving CPU and IOPS.
• **Unified Invalidation:** Tag-based eviction lets you invalidate groups of related entries across both L1 and L2 simultaneously with minimal code.
• **Optimized Serialization:** Offloads heavy payload serialization to the distributed L2 tier while keeping fast allocations lean.

### Watch Out

Be mindful of L1 memory consumption. Because `HybridCache` holds references in process RAM, caching excessively large object graphs without configuring proper size limits can trigger Gen 2 GC spikes.

